Free the knotes when a kqueue is torn down, because ours hold DESCRIPTORS.

filter_unregister_all destroyed each filter and left its knotes in place behind an XXX-FIXME.
Upstream can afford that: on macOS a kqueue registration costs no descriptor, so an orphaned knote
is memory. THIS PORT DUPS EVERY WATCHED DESCRIPTOR (_dup_4libkqueue), so an orphaned knote is a
LEAKED FILE DESCRIPTOR, and when the leaked descriptor is the write end of a pipe, that pipe can
never report end of file to anyone.

THAT IS THE HANG. Measured, with a dup and close ledger, in CMake:

  kqdup fd=21 -> 24        the two watched descriptors are dup'd
  kqdup fd=25 -> 27
  spawn=act i=1 type=2 fd=25 newfd=1     and 25 and 21 become the child's stdout and stderr
  spawn=act i=2 type=2 fd=21 newfd=2
  ... 24 and 27 are NEVER closed ...

The application then closes its own copies and waits for the child's output to end. It cannot:
our two dups still hold the write ends open. Reading the hung process fd table from inside the
container shows exactly that, a write end beside every watched read end on the same pipe inode.
The kqueue those knotes belonged to had already been closed, which is why the application's later
EV_DELETE for the same descriptors found no entry: the knotes were orphaned, not deleted.

knote_delete_all walks a filter's knote tree and deletes each one, which is what closes kn_dupfd
through the filter's own delete. It runs BEFORE kf_destroy, because the delete still needs the
filter and the epoll descriptor that destroy tears down, and it stops on refusal rather than
spinning, since knote_delete only fails on a double delete.

VERIFIED: a three line CMake script whose body is one execute_process hung every run before and
completes now, with the child's output captured (23.4.0). cmake -S -B now runs system and compiler
detection to completion instead of blocking forever in kevent.

--- a/src/common/filter.c
